ility AC), BRANCH-A-E006 (operator pattern aligns), noted in E003/migration\n- REQ-11 (Open question) migration path to production vector DB → E003 (+ BRANCH-A-E007 if affirmed)\n\nEvery requirement covered. ✓\n\nNow write epic bodies. Each epic file body: Title (one line), Description, Scope, Acceptance Criteria, HLR Traceability (+ risks where relevant). No cross-references to sibling epic IDs in bodies! Important: \"Do not reference other epics by draft ID (E001, E003) anywhere in the body\". Hmm — but HLR Traceability sections reference requirement IDs, fine. But my epic bodies must NOT say \"E001\" etc. Also must not reference branch IDs. Describe dependencies in plain terms (\"the provisioning approach decision\", \"the scoping model decision\"). Careful in ACs too.\n\nWait — but the epic body \"HLR Traceability\" section: lists strategy HLRs — requirement labels like \"REQ-1\" are fine (they're strategy requirements, not sibling epics).\n\nLet me now define signals files. One per epic: `RHAISTRAT-8004-E001-ai-signals.md` etc. with markdown table Signal/Value/Rationale. For investigations, the 5 investigation signals; for implementations, the 9.\n\nNow let me think about whether frontmatter `set` handles dotted keys (ai_signals.change_specificity=1) — per the prompt example, yes.\n\nAlso: title in frontmatter — set via script (title=\"<epic title>\"). And body has \"# Title\" line.\n\nLet me also double check `frontmatter.py set` on a body-only file works. I'll write the decomposition body first and test.\n\nLet me now write all content. I'll be careful and thorough but concise enough.\n\nFirst, the decomposition summary body:\n\n---\n\n# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8004 — Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio\n\n## Decomposition Inputs and Precedence\n\n- full strategy attachment missing note\n- Staff Engineer Input parsed first and takes precedence; overrides logged below.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Override Log\n| # | Strategy position (AI-generated) | Staff Engineer correction | Effect on decomposition |\n1. OGX auto-provisions PostgreSQL → OGX defaults to SQLite; BFF hardcodes SQLite; PostgreSQL is new work → provisioning epics created; no \"extend existing infrastructure\" assumption.\n2. Custom image build + Konflux onboarding (1-2 sprints) → rhel9/postgresql-16 includes pgvector; no image build, no Konflux onboarding → no repo-onboarding/license-validation/konflux-onboarding epics; onboarding chain rules (5-10, 25) not applicable.\n3. Platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC → not available until RHOAI 3.6; for 3.5 Gen AI Studio must self-provision → 3.5-scoped provisioning with portability AC.\n4. Self-provisioning needs RHOAI-controlled operator → hard dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164 (in progress); go/no-go gate in sprint planning → Investigation epic + conditional branches (operator-based / BFF-managed / BYO).\n5. Auto Rag in affected components/ACs → decoupled; Auto Rag pgvector compatibility moved to RHAISTRAT-7824 → Auto Rag AC struck; validation scoped to Gen AI Studio playground only.\n6. Latency/corpus/index open questions → resolved as dev-experimentation scope; same-order-of-magnitude benchmark; flat scan → benchmark epic with fixed criterion; those open questions not modeled as investigations.\n7. Vector/application storage consolidation + SQLite→PostgreSQL migration concerns → vector_io provider config only; application storage remains SQLite → no migration epic for app storage.\n8. Reusable remote vector store implementation → majority of effort is provisioning → no OGX distribution code epic; OGX is a passive component (validated via AC).\n\n## Epic List\n(table: ID, title, type, team, priority, component)\n\n## Dependency DAG\nMermaid.\n\n## DAG Justification\ntable.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\ntable.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\n## Conditional Branches\ndocument branches.\n\n---\n\nNote epic_count: main-plan 5 or all 9 — \"may count either main-plan epics only or all epics including branches\". I'll use 9 and say so, or 5? I'll set epic_count=9 and state main-plan=5 in the body. Hmm — critical_path_length=2 (main plan). Let me double check: main-plan critical path: E001→E005 (2), E002→E005 (2), E004→E005 (2). Yes 2.\n\nNow epic bodies. Let me draft each.\n\n### E001 — Investigation: RHOAI 3.5 default vector store provisioning approach go/no-go\n\nBody:\n\n# Go/No-Go Decision: RHOAI 3.5 Default Vector Store Provisioning Approach\n\n## Description\nResolves which provisioning approach ships the default PostgreSQL/pgvector vector store for the Gen AI Studio playground in RHOAI 3.5: (A) operator-based self-provisioning in the RHOAI-controlled Gen AI Studio operator layer delivered by the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164); (B) gen-ai BFF-managed provisioning of the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance; or (C) requiring customers to bring their own PostgreSQL with manual OGXServer configuration. The outcome selects one of three conditional implementation branches and is recorded as the go/no-go gate the development team defines in sprint planning.\n\n## Scope\n- Confirm RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) delivery timing against the RHOAI 3.5 schedule — the hard dependency for operator-based self-provisioning.\n- Evaluate the two fallbacks if the operator layer is not ready: BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning (preferred alternative) and customer-provided PostgreSQL with manual OGXServer CR configuration.\n- Define the go/no-go decision criteria and gate timing (sprint planning checkpoint).\n- Produce a decision record: selected approach, rationale, risks, and portability implications toward the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided shared PostgreSQL.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A decision record exists naming the selected provisioning approach for RHOAI 3.5 with rationale grounded in RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery status.\n- The go/no-go gate criteria and checkpoint timing are documented and agreed in sprint planning.\n- Each alternative outcome has a defined conditional implementation branch (operator-based, BFF-managed, bring-your-own) so implementation can start immediately after the decision.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- REQ-9 (Staff Engineer Input: go/no-go gate defined as part of sprint planning).\n- Enables REQ-1 (out-of-the-box default vector store) by selecting the delivery approach.\n\nRisks/notes: release commitment risk — SE notes it will be challenging to commit this strategy to a specific release until RHAISTRAT-7164 is delivered.\n\n### E002 — Investigation: default vector store scoping model\n\n# Scoping Model for the Default Vector Store\n\n## Description\nDetermines the scoping model for the default pgvector-backed vector store in multi-tenant environments — per namespace, per OGX (Llama Stack) instance, or both — and what that means for isolation, resource overhead, and lifecycle ownership. The Gen AI Studio lead prefers per-namespace isolation; the per-instance concern originally stemmed from Auto Rag, which is now out of scope (addressed in RHAISTRAT-7824).\n\n## Scope\n- Analyze how playground OGXServer instances map to namespaces in the current gen-ai BFF provisioning model.\n- Evaluate per-namespace vs per-instance vs hybrid scoping: isolation guarantees, PostgreSQL instance proliferation, resource overhead in constrained environments, lifecycle ownership.\n- Recommend a scoping model with the isolation and resource rationale, consumed by the provisioning implementation and validation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A decision record recommends one scoping model (or a supported combination) with isolation and resource-trade-off rationale.\n- The recommendation addresses multi-tenant isolation (the strategy's per-namespace example) and resource overhead in resource-constrained environments.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- REQ-5 (default vector store scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments, e.g. per-namespace isolation).\n\n### E003 — Investigation: migration path decision\n\n# Migration Path from the Default Vector Store to Production Vector Databases\n\n## Description\nDecides whether users need an explicit migration path from the default (development-experimentation-grade) vector store to production-grade vector databases, and in what form — given the strategy direction that users with production-scale vector workloads should use an external vector database, and the stakeholder concern about aligning with commonly-used vector DB providers for easier migration.\n\n## Scope\n- Determine what migration would mean for the default store's typical usage (small document sets, dev experimentation): e.g., re-ingestion against a new vector database vs data export.\n- Assess alignment with commonly-used vector DB providers supported by the OGX vector I/O provider surface.\n- Recommend: provide a migration path (and its form) or document guidance only (default store positioned as dev-experimentation-grade; production workloads directed to external vector databases).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A recommendation exists with rationale, covering the product expectation for users outgrowing the default store.\n- If a migration path is affirmed, its form and scope are specified sufficiently to plan the follow-up implementation.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- REQ-11 (open question: migration path from the default vector store to a production-grade vector database).\n\n### E004 — Implementation: benchmark\n\n# Inline Milvus Baseline and pgvector Default Vector Store Benchmark\n\n## Description\nCaptures the performance baseline of the current inline Milvus vector store provider and benchmarks the remote pgvector vector I/O provider against it on representative Gen AI Studio playground RAG workloads, verifying pgvector is in the same order of magnitude. Also records PostgreSQL resource-sizing observations against the model-registry-operator's resource requests/limits starting point. This is Tier 1 risk-reduction work: it does not depend on the provisioning approach decision and must capture the inline baseline before inline providers are removed from the OGX distribution image (RHAISTRAT-7474).\n\n## Scope\n- Capture the inline Milvus provider baseline on representative playground RAG workloads (small document sets).\n- Benchmark the OGX remote pgvector vector I/O provider (flat scan, no index — sufficient for the default dev-experimentation store) on the same workloads, backed by a standalone PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector — no custom image build).\n- Compare results: verify same order of magnitude (no strict latency SLAs — p50/p95/p99 thresholds are explicitly not required).\n- Record resource usage observations for PostgreSQL/pgvector sizing relative to the model-registry-operator's resource requests/limits reference.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Inline Milvus baseline captured and documented before inline providers are removed from the OGX distribution (coordination with RHAISTRAT-7474 noted as a risk, not an AC on external timing). Hmm — careful: AC must be testable on THIS epic's deliverable. \"The benchmark report documents the inline Milvus baseline\" ✓; \"report documents pgvector measurements on identical workloads and the same-order-of-magnitude comparison\" ✓; \"report records resource observations vs the model-registry reference sizing\" ✓. The \"before inline removal\" is timing — put in Risks, and AC phrased as \"baseline captured from the current inline provider while still available\".\n- Benchmark harness is reproducible (scripts/config retained).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- REQ-6 (lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments — sizing observations).\n- Contributes to REQ-2 (no loss of capability — performance parity evidence).\n\nRisks: baseline must be captured while the inline provider still exists in the distribution — cross-strategy timing risk with RHAISTRAT-7474.\n\n### E005 — Implementation: E2E validation\n\n# End-to-End Validation of Gen AI Studio Playground RAG with the Default Vector Store\n\n## Description\nValidates, end to end, that Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default pgvector-backed vector store with no loss of current capability, once the selected provisioning approach has delivered an instance. Verifies the out-of-the-box experience (no manual setup), multi-tenant scoping, decoupling (separate process, not inline), and performance consistent with the benchmark findings.\n\n## Scope\n- Execute playground RAG workflows (document ingestion, retrieval, generation) against the default vector store delivered by the selected provisioning approach.\n- Verify no loss of current capability vs the inline-provider experience (functional parity checklist + benchmark evidence).\n- Verify multi-tenant scoping behavior per the selected scoping model.\n- Verify the default store runs as a separate process decoupled from the OGX instance.\n- Verify no additional manual setup steps are required for RAG workflows (out-of-the-box experience).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Validation executed against an instance delivered by the selected provisioning approach (available and configured in the target namespace) — AC gate, not a schedule claim.\n- Functional parity: all playground RAG capabilities work with the default store as with the current inline provider.\n- Multi-tenant scoping verified per the selected scoping model.\n- Validation report published with results and any follow-ups.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- REQ-2, REQ-4, REQ-5, REQ-7.\n\n### BRANCH-A-E006 — operator-based provisioning (outcome A: RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time)\n\n# Operator-Based Self-Provisioning of the Default pgvector Vector Store\n\n## Description\nDelivers the default vector store by extending the RHOAI-controlled Gen AI Studio operator layer (delivered by the dashboard operator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164) with PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management, following the model-registry-operator pattern: the operator provisions and manages a PostgreSQL instance (existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector — no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding) and the gen-ai BFF configures playground OGXServer instances to use the remote pgvector vector I/O provider backed by it.\n\n## Scope\n- Operator-layer PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management (provisioning, connection secret, upgrades, cleanup) following the model-registry-operator pattern, using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image that ships pgvector.\n- Resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing, adjusted per benchmark findings.\n- Implement the scoping model selected by the scoping investigation (e.g., per-namespace isolation).\n- Gen-ai BFF wiring: playground OGXServer instances are configured with the remote pgvector vector I/O provider connected to the operator-provisioned database; OGX application storage remains on its existing SQLite default (vector storage and application storage are independent configurations).\n- Reuse the existing OGX remote vector store implementation — no OGX distribution code change expected; validate provider behavior as an acceptance criterion.\n- Document the default store's positioning (development-experimentation grade; production workloads directed to external vector databases).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A PostgreSQL/pgvector instance is provisioned and managed by the operator with credentials delivered via secret; lifecycle (provision, cleanup) is handled without manual intervention.\n- Playground OGXServer instances use the remote pgvector vector I/O provider backed by the provisioned database; application storage remains SQLite.\n- Resource requests/limits follow the model-registry-operator starting point, adjusted per the benchmark findings.\n- Scoping follows the selected scoping model with per-namespace isolation unless the decision differs.\n- Escape hatch: the playground can be pointed at an externally provisioned vector database (rollback path when inline providers are removed).\n- No OGX distribution code change is required; if a gap is found in the remote pgvector provider, it is documented and resolved (fix-and-retry).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- REQ-1, REQ-2, REQ-4, REQ-5, REQ-6, REQ-10 (portability: operator pattern aligns with consuming a platform-provided shared database in RHOAI 3.6).\n\ngate_failure_impact: action=rewrite, fallback: if the operator layer is not available in time for RHOAI 3.5, provisioning moves to the BFF-managed or bring-your-own branch; the operator-based lifecycle design does not ship in 3.5 (may move to 3.6 aligned with the platform-provided shared database).\n\n### BRANCH-B-E006 — BFF-managed provisioning (outcome B)\n\n# BFF-Managed Provisioning of the Default pgvector Vector Store\n\n## Description\nFallback delivery when the RHOAI-controlled operator layer is not available in time: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly — creating the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured and no instance exists in the namespace — similar to how the BFF currently provisions guardrails infrastructure. The BFF also configures playground OGXServer instances to use the remote pgvector vector I/O provider backed by that instance.\n\n## Scope\n- BFF provisioning of PostgreSQL (existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector; no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding) when a playground with RAG is configured, if none exists in the namespace.\n- Explicit lifecycle design — the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, and credential rotation need explicit design attention.\n- Portability by design: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n- Implement the scoping model selected by the scoping investigation (per-namespace preferred).\n- Resource sizing from the model-registry-operator reference, adjusted per benchmark findings.\n- OGXServer wiring: remote pgvector vector I/O provider; application storage remains SQLite.\n- Document positioning (dev-experimentation grade; external DB for production).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Configuring a playground with RAG provisions a PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret) in the namespace when none exists; the OGX vector I/O provider is configured to use it.\n- Lifecycle behaviors (cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart handling, credential rotation) are designed and implemented per the lifecycle design.\n- Portability: the connection-resolution path is abstracted so a platform-provided shared connection string can be consumed in RHOAI 3.6 without rework.\n- Scoping per the selected model; resource sizing per reference + benchmark.\n- Escape hatch: externally provisioned vector database can be used instead.\n- Application storage remains SQLite (no consolidation with vector storage).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- REQ-1, REQ-2, REQ-4, REQ-5, REQ-6, REQ-10.\n\ngate_failure_impact: action=remove — if the operator-based approach is selected (operator layer lands in time) or the team chooses customer-provided PostgreSQL, this epic is not implemented.\n\n### BRANCH-C-E006 — BYO (outcome C)\n\n# Customer-Provided PostgreSQL Support for Playground RAG\n\n## Description\nDegraded fallback: require customers to bring their own PostgreSQL for RAG capabilities in the playground. Users manually configure the OGXServer CR spec with their PostgreSQL connection details (aligning with the breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0), with the playground abstracting OGX where possible.\n\n## Scope\n- Support user-supplied PostgreSQL connection details (connectionString secret) on playground OGXServer instances, with the remote pgvector vector I/O provider configured against the user's database.\n- Validation/error surfacing in the playground when connection details are missing or invalid (RAG requires a vector store once inline providers are removed).\n- User-facing documentation for configuring a PostgreSQL database with pgvector, aligned with the OGX 1.0 breaking-change posture.\n- Document positioning: guidance for choosing a production-grade vector database.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A user-configured PostgreSQL connection (via secret) is honored by the playground's OGXServer instance; RAG workflows function against it.\n- Missing/invalid configuration produces actionable feedback in the playground.\n- Documentation covers setup end to end with no undocumented manual steps.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- REQ-2 (playground RAG functions — with user-provided store), REQ-4 (separate process). Note: this branch deliberately does not satisfy REQ-1/REQ-7's no-manual-setup expectation for 3.5 — accepted degradation per the Staff Engineer fallback.\n\ngate_failure_impact: action=remove — if self-provisioning (operator-based or BFF-managed) is selected, this epic is not implemented; only the external-database escape-hatch support may be retained.\n\n### BRANCH-A-E007 — migration path deliverable (outcome A of migration decision)\n\n# Migration Path from the Default Vector Store to External Vector Databases\n\n## Description\nProvides the migration path affirmed by the migration-path decision: a supported way for users to move from the default development-experimentation vector store to a production-grade vector database, aligned with commonly-used vector DB providers.\n\n## Scope\n- Implement/support the affirmed migration form (e.g., re-ingestion of source documents against a new vector database, or data export), per the decision's specification.\n- Align with commonly-used vector DB providers supported by the OGX vector I/O provider surface.\n- User documentation for the migration path.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- The affirmed migration form works end to end for a representative playground document set.\n- Documentation covers the migration steps for at least the commonly-used providers identified in the decision.\n- Technical review against the delivered implementation completed (accuracy validated). Hmm — that's the docs AC (Rule 24 applies to docs-authoring epics; this is general Implementation — skip the mandatory phrasing but a review AC is fine). I'll include \"documented steps are validated against the delivered capability\".\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- REQ-11.\n\ngate_failure_impact: action=remove — if the decision is \"no migration path\", only positioning guidance is documented (folded into the delivered default-store epic) and this epic does not exist.\n\nNow signals files (one per epic, 9 files). Format: markdown table Signal | Value | Rationale.\n\nNow frontmatter for each epic:\n\nE001: epic_id=RHAISTRAT-8004-E001, title=\"Go/no-go decision: RHOAI 3.5 default vector store provisioning approach\", parent_strat=RHAISTRAT-8004, component=\"Gen AI Studio\", team=\"Gen AI Studio\", type=Investigation, priority=P0, dependencies=\"\" (none — set nothing? dependencies default [] — omit or set empty). I'll omit dependencies for no-dep epics. investigation_signals.* per above: question_specificity=1, source_accessibility=0, local_runnability=0, cluster_hardware_dependence=0, human_judgment_required=-2.\n\nE002: Investigation P0, inv signals: 1,1,0,-1,0.\n\nE003: Investigation P1, inv signals: 1,1,0,0,-2.\n\nE004: Implementation P0, ai_signals: change_specificity=1, pattern_precedent=0, adapter_pattern=0, existing_foundation=1, open_questions=1, external_dependency=0, human_process_gates=1, repo_access=1, architecture_claims=1.\n\nE005: Implementation P0, deps=\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E001,RHAISTRAT-8004-E002,RHAISTRAT-8004-E004\", gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8004-E001, gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite, gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach=\"...\", ai_signals: 1,1,0,1,0,1,1,1,1.\n\nBRANCH-A-E006: Implementation P0, branch=A, gated_by=E001, dependencies=\"RHAISTRAT-8004-