{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-36ad4710-fa30-4aad-b6db-d17a8e85ac73","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9136f0c327ece978","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-62sgyse7/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.md","content":"# Go/No-Go Decision: RHOAI 3.5 Default Vector Store Provisioning Approach\n\n## Description\n\nResolves which provisioning approach ships the default PostgreSQL/pgvector vector store for the Gen AI Studio playground in RHOAI 3.5. Three bounded outcomes: (A) operator-based self-provisioning in the RHOAI-controlled Gen AI Studio operator layer delivered by the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164); (B) gen-ai BFF-managed provisioning of the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance; or (C) requiring customers to bring their own PostgreSQL with manual OGXServer configuration. The outcome selects one of three conditional implementation branches and is recorded as the go/no-go gate the development team defines in sprint planning.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Confirm RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress) delivery timing against the RHOAI 3.5 schedule — the hard dependency for operator-based self-provisioning, since the upstream ogx-k8s-operator cannot carry RHOAI-specific provisioning logic.\n- Evaluate the two fallbacks if the operator layer is not ready in time: BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning (the preferred alternative) and customer-provided PostgreSQL with manual OGXServer CR configuration (aligned with the OGX 1.0 breaking-change posture).\n- Define the go/no-go decision criteria and the sprint-planning checkpoint at which the gate is called.\n- Produce a decision record: selected approach, rationale, risks, and portability implications toward the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A decision record exists naming the selected RHOAI 3.5 provisioning approach, with rationale grounded in the confirmed RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery status.\n- The go/no-go gate criteria and checkpoint timing are documented and agreed in sprint planning.\n- Each of the three possible outcomes has a defined conditional implementation branch (operator-based, BFF-managed, bring-your-own) so implementation can start immediately once the decision is made.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- REQ-9: go/no-go gate for the 3.5 approach defined as part of sprint planning (Staff Engineer Input).\n- Enables REQ-1 (out-of-the-box default vector store) and REQ-8 (RHAISTRAT-7474 unblocked) by selecting the delivery approach.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Release-commitment risk: per the Staff Engineer Input, it will be challenging to commit this strategy to a specific release until RHAISTRAT-7164 is delivered; this decision epic exists precisely to manage that risk rather than let it silently block all work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":101639,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bcdcfbfb0101f62c","agent_id":"a064da097f30cb08b","session_id":"172d32aa-435a-4a07-8b59-185510da7cc9","uuid":"8497f75d-2d3b-42c2-9b29-36e31cc3dcf0","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:34:53.379Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
