erator's PostgreSQL sizing and are adjusted per the benchmark findings.\n- OGXServer wiring: playground OGXServer instances use the remote pgvector vector I/O provider backed by the provisioned instance; OGX application storage remains on its existing SQLite default (independent configurations — no consolidation).\n- Reuse the existing OGX remote vector store implementation; no OGX distribution code change expected.\n- Document the default store's positioning: development-experimentation grade, with production-scale workloads directed to an external vector database.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Configuring a playground with RAG provisions a PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret) in the namespace when none exists, and the OGX vector I/O provider is configured to use it.\n- Lifecycle behaviors — cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart handling, credential rotation — are designed and implemented per the lifecycle design.\n- Portability: the connection-resolution path is abstracted so a platform-provided shared connection string can be consumed in RHOAI 3.6 without rework.\n- Scoping follows the selected scoping model; resource requests/limits follow the model-registry-operator reference, adjusted per the benchmark findings.\n- Escape hatch / rollback path: the playground can be pointed at an externally provisioned vector database.\n- OGX application storage remains SQLite; no vector/application storage consolidation is introduced.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- REQ-1: default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning or configuration.\n- REQ-2: playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store.\n- REQ-4: default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance.\n- REQ-5: multi-tenant scoping per the selected model.\n- REQ-6: lightweight resource footprint.\n- REQ-10: portability toward the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided shared PostgreSQL (explicit design-for-portability requirement).\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This branch exists only if the go/no-go decision selects the BFF-managed fallback (operator layer not ready in time).\n- Epic size: ~1.5-2 sprints (qualitative — the strategy's effort arithmetic is unavailable); kept whole because BFF provisioning and provider wiring form a single logical change over the same BFF code paths. Re-split trigger: the lifecycle-design work (cleanup, pod-restart handling, credential rotation) exceeds its design estimate by more than ~50%, at which point lifecycle hardening splits into a follow-up epic within this branch.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":105928,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bcdcfbfb0101f62c","agent_id":"a064da097f30cb08b","session_id":"172d32aa-435a-4a07-8b59-185510da7cc9","uuid":"66cbfe31-8371-4a7c-92df-f4081f5f69e3","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:35:49.098Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
