of existing infrastructure) | OGX defaults to SQLite (`storage.kv.type: sqlite`, `storage.sql.type: sqlite`); the gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite; PostgreSQL is new work, not an extension | Provisioning is modeled as new implementation work (conditional branches), not an extension epic |\n37\t| 2 | Custom pgvector image build + Konflux onboarding estimated 1-2 sprints | `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` already includes pgvector; model-registry-operator already auto-provisions it; no image build, no Konflux onboarding | No `repo-onboarding` / `license-validation` / `konflux-onboarding` epics; onboarding-chain rules (5-10, 25) are not applicable |\n38\t| 3 | (implied platform-level database) | Platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC is a RHOAI 3.6 direction; for 3.5 Gen AI Studio must provision its own instance | 3.5-scoped provisioning with a portability AC toward the 3.6 shared database |\n39\t| 4 | Self-provisioning via operator | Requires the RHOAI-controlled operator from RHAISTRAT-7164 (In Progress) — a hard dependency with uncertain timing; go/no-go gate defined in sprint planning; fallbacks are customer-provided PostgreSQL or BFF-managed provisioning | Investigation epic (go/no-go) with three conditional implementation branches (operator-based / BFF-managed / bring-your-own) |\n40\t| 5 | Auto Rag is an affected component with its own acceptance criterion | Decouple from Auto Rag — its Gen AI Studio integration targets RHOAI 3.6 in RHAISTRAT-7824; this strategy validates pgvector only for Gen AI Studio Playground RAG | Auto Rag acceptance criterion struck (REQ-3, override logged); no AutoRAG epics; validation scoped to the playground |\n41\t| 6 | Open questions on latency thresholds, corpus size ranges, index type | Resolved: dev-experimentation scope; benchmark verifies same order of magnitude vs inline Milvus baseline; flat scan (no index) is sufficient; strict SLAs not required | Benchmark epic with a fixed comparison criterion; these open questions are NOT modeled as investigations |\n42\t| 7 | Consolidating vector and application storage; SQLite-to-PostgreSQL upgrade path | Vector storage (`remote::pgvector` via `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`) and application storage are independent configurations; application storage stays SQLite | No storage-consolidation or app-storage migration epic; \"application storage remains SQLite\" is an AC on every delivery branch |\n43\t| 8 | (effort on vector_io provider integration) | Existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable; majority of effort is PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning | No OGX distribution code epic; OGX is a passive component validated via ACs on the delivery branches |\n44\t\n45\t## Epic List\n46\t\n47\tMain plan (5 epics):\n48\t\n49\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Component |\n50\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|-----------|\n51\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Go/no-go decision: RHOAI 3.5 default vector store provisioning approach | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | Gen AI Studio |\n52\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Scoping model for the default vector store | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | Gen AI Studio |\n53\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Migration path from the default vector store to production vector databases | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P1 | Gen AI Studio |\n54\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | Inline Milvus baseline and pgvector default vector store benchmark | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | Gen AI Studio |\n55\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E005 | End-to-end validation of Gen AI Studio playground RAG with the default vector store | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | Gen AI Studio |\n56\t\n57\tConditional branch epics (4 — not standalone Jira issues; attached to their gating\n58\tInvestigation as a plan document):\n59\t\n60\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Component | Gated by |\n61\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|-----------|----------|\n62\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E006 | Operator-based self-provisioning of the default pgvector vector store | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | Gen AI Studio | E001 (outcome A: RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time) |\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E006 | BFF-managed provisioning of the default pgvector vector store | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | Gen AI Studio | E001 (outcome B: operator layer slips, BFF-managed fallback) |\n64\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E006 | Customer-provided PostgreSQL support for playground RAG | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | Gen AI Studio | E001 (outcome C: bring-your-own fallback) |\n65\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E007 | Migration path deliverable: default vector store to external vector databases | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P1 | Gen AI Studio | E003 (outcome A: migration path affirmed) |\n66\t\n67\t## Dependency DAG\n68\t\n69\tMain plan (branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path):\n70\t\n71\t```mermaid\n72\tgraph TD\n73\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: provisioning approach go/no-go\"]\n74\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: scoping model\"]\n75\t    E003[\"E003 Investigation: migration path decision\"]\n76\t    E004[\"E004 Implementation: inline Milvus baseline + pgvector benchmark\"]\n77\t    E005[\"E005 Implementation: end-to-end playground RAG validation\"]\n78\t\n79\t    E001 --> E005\n80\t    E002 --> E005\n81\t    E004 --> E005\n82\t```\n83\t\n84\tConditional branches (dashed semantics — exactly one E001 outcome is selected; the\n85\tE003 branch exists only if the migration path is affirmed):\n86\t\n87\t```mermaid\n88\tgraph TD\n89\t    E001[\"E001 go/no-go\"] --> BA[\"BRANCH-A-E006 operator-based provisioning\"]\n90\t    E001 --> BB[\"BRANCH-B-E006 BFF-managed provisioning\"]\n91\t    E001 --> BC[\"BRANCH-C-E006 customer-provided PostgreSQL\"]\n92\t    E002[\"E002 scoping model\"] --> BA\n93\t    E002 --> BB\n94\t    E002 --> BC\n95\t    E003[\"E003 migration decision\"] --> B7[\"BRANCH-A-E007 migration path deliverable\"]\n96\t```\n97\t\n98\t## DAG Justification\n99\t\n100\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n101\t|------|------|-----------|\n102\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The provisioning-approach decision is a true gate for validation: the approach is selected, its branch delivers, and validation runs against the selected delivery. The branch-completion coupling is expressed as an AC on E005 (\"validated against an instance delivered by the selected provisioning approach\"), never as an edge to a branch epic. |\n103\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 | The scoping model determines the multi-tenant validation topology (per-namespace vs per-instance test design). |\n104\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 12 (artifact consumer) | The validation consumes the benchmark's inline-Milvus baseline, harness, and sizing findings as its no-loss-of-capability comparison substrate. |\n105\t| E001 → BRANCH-A/B/C-E006 | Rule 3 + conditional decomposition | Bounded outcomes (≤3): operator-based, BFF-managed, bring-your-own. Each branch is a true gate — `remove` for the outcomes not selected, `rewrite` for the selected outcome's own fallback. |\n106\t| E002 → BRANCH-A/B/C-E006 | Rule 3 | The scoping model rewrites what each provisioning branch implements (where the instance lives, isolation, lifecycle ownership). |\n107\t| E003 → BRANCH-A-E007 | Rule 3 + conditional decomposition | Bounded outcome (provide a migration path or not). `remove` if not affirmed — positioning guidance only. |\n108\t| No edge E001 ↔ E002, E001 ↔ E003, E002 ↔ E003 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Independent investigations with no shared artifacts; each resolves a different unknown. |\n109\t| No edge E004 → branches | Rule 13 carve-out (configuration reference) | Benchmark sizing findings adjust resource requests/limits — an AC gate on each branch (\"sizing follows the model-registry-operator reference, adjusted per benchmark findings\"), not a DAG dependency. Keeps Tier 1 benchmark work parallel with gated branch work. |\n110\t| No edge branches → E005 | Branch convention | Branch epics are not main-plan nodes; a main-plan epic must never depend on a branch. E005 depends on the Investigation (E001) and carries the branch-completion coupling as an AC. |\n111\t| No onboarding-chain edges (rules 5-10, 25) | Not applicable | Staff Engineer Correction 2: the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image ships pgvector — no image build, no Konflux onboarding, no repo onboarding, no license validation anywhere in this decomposition. |\n112\t\n113\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n114\t\n115\tRequirement inventory: the HLR table is in the unavailable strategy attachment, so the\n116\tstrategy's Acceptance Criteria (AC) and Success Criteria (SC) are used, plus\n117\tStaff-Engineer-derived requirements (SE). Full coverage, no orphans:\n118\t\n119\t| Requirement | Source | Priority | Epic(s) |\n120\t|-------------|--------|----------|---------|\n121\t| REQ-1: default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning/configuration | AC | P0 | BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006 (full); BRANCH-C-E006 (deliberate partial — accepted degradation per Staff Engineer fallback, see note) |\n122\t| REQ-2: Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with no loss of current capability | AC | P0 | BRANCH-A/B/C-E006 (delivery), E004 (performance evidence), E005 (functional validation) |\n123\t| REQ-3: Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | AC | — | **Struck per Staff Engineer Input** (override #5): Auto Rag pgvector compatibility is RHAISTRAT-7824 scope (RHOAI 3.6). No epic in this decomposition. |\n124\t| REQ-4: default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance (not inline) | AC | P0 | BRANCH-A/B/C-E006 (remote pgvector provider by design), E005 (verification) |\n125\t| REQ-5: scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (e.g., per-namespace isolation) | AC | P0 | E002 (decision), BRANCH-A/B/C-E006 (implementation), E005 (verification) |\n126\t| REQ-6: lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments | AC | P0 | E004 (sizing observations), BRANCH-A/B/C-E006 (resource sizing from model-registry-operator reference, adjusted per benchmark) |\n127\t| REQ-7: no functional regression and no additional manual setup vs the current experience | SC | P0 | E005 (regression check), BRANCH-A/B-E006 (no manual setup); BRANCH-C-E006 is the documented exception (fallback) |\n128\t| REQ-8: RHAISTRAT-7474 unblocked | SC | P0 | Whole decomposition: E001 (decision) + selected E006 branch (replacement capability) |\n129\t| REQ-9: go/no-go gate for the 3.5 approach defined as part of sprint planning | SE | P0 | E001 |\n130\t| REQ-10: portability toward the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided shared PostgreSQL | SE | P1 | BRANCH-B-E006 (explicit portability AC), BRANCH-A-E006 (operator pattern aligns with consuming a shared connection string) |\n131\t| REQ-11: migration path from the default vector store to a production-grade vector database | Open question | P1 | E003 (decision), BRANCH-A-E007 (deliverable, only if affirmed) |\n132\t\n133\tNo lower-priority HLR is kept bundled inside a higher-priority epic: the only sub-P0\n134\trequirement (REQ-11) has its own investigation and conditional branch.\n135\t\n136\t## Health Warnings\n137\t\n138\t1. **Missing strategy sections (input health).** The full strategy is a Jira attachment\n139\t   (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) not present in the workspace, so the Affected\n140\t   Components, Impacted Teams, HLR, Dependencies, and NFR tables were unavailable. The\n141\t   decomposition is built from the summary, acceptance criteria, open questions, and\n142\t   Staff Engineer Input (which takes precedence). Component and team assignments are\n143\t   inferred (Gen AI Studio throughout — the SE input scopes every work stream to Gen AI\n144\t   Studio after decoupling Auto Rag); verify against the full strategy when the\n145\t   attachment is available.\n146\t2. **Schedule risk on a Blocker-priority strategy (not a priority inversion).**\n147\t   RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) is a hard dependency with\n148\t   uncertain timing for the primary (operator-based) approach; the SE notes the strategy\n149\t   cannot commit to a release until it resolves. Modeled as the E001 go/no-go\n150\t   investigation with three conditional branches rather than a serial blocking edge, so\n151\t   Tier 1 work (benchmark, investigations) proceeds in parallel.\n152\t3. **Cross-strategy timing risk on baseline capture.** The inline Milvus baseline must\n153\t   be captured while inline providers still exist in the OGX distribution image\n154\t   (RHAISTRAT-7474 removes them). The benchmark epic is un-gated Tier 1 work partly for\n155\t   this reason; if RHAISTRAT-7474 lands first, the baseline must be reconstructed from a\n156\t   pinned pre-removal image.\n157\t4. **Scope trap avoided (SE override).** The \"Auto Rag functions with the default vector\n158\t   store\" AC contradicts the Staff Engineer Input's decoupling; it is struck, not\n159\t   decomposed. If Auto Rag re-enters this strategy's scope, the per-instance scoping\n160\t   option and an Auto Rag validation epic need re-evaluation (see E002).\n161\t5. **Epic size above the ~2-week guideline (managed).** BRANCH-A-E006 and\n162\t   BRANCH-B-E006 are each estimated at ~1.5-2 sprints (the strategy's own effort\n163\t   arithmetic is in the missing attachment, so these are qualitative). Kept whole\n164\t   because each is one logical change — provision and wire the default store for one\n165\t   approach — sharing the same code paths, CRD/BFF surfaces, and review cycle; splitting\n166\t   provisioning from provider wiring would build the same surface twice and add an\n167\t   artificial serialization edge. Re-split trigger: if the lifecycle-design work\n168\t   (cleanup on deletion, pod-restart handling, credential rotation) exceeds its initial\n169\t   design estimate by more than ~50%, split lifecycle hardening into a follow-up epic\n170\t   inside the same branch.\n171\t6. **Architecture-context gap on the image claim.** The SE's claims that\n172\t   `rhel9/postgresql-16` ships pgvector and that `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` is\n173\t   already in the rhods-operator CSV are not corroborated by the available\n174\t   architecture-context files (the model-registry-operator context does corroborate\n175\t   operator-pattern PostgreSQL auto-provisioning with per-instance postgres deployments).\n176\t   No epic depends on the image-build claim — no image-build work exists in this\n177\t   decomposition — but verify the image contents during branch implementation.\n178\t\n179\t## Tiered Delivery\n180\t\n181\tThe external dependency with uncertain timing (RHAISTRAT-7164, another in-progress\n182\tstrategy) was evaluated for tiered delivery per Step 5.5. Partial value IS deliverable\n183\twithout it:\n184\t\n185\t- **Tier 1 (independent, starts immediately):** E001, E002, E003 (decisions) and E004\n186\t  (risk-reduction benchmark — captures the inline baseline before inline-provider removal\n187\t  and de-risks the pgvector order-of-magnitude question).\n188\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** BRANCH-A/B/C-E006 (exactly one selected provisioning\n189\t  branch), E005 (end-to-end validation), BRANCH-A-E007 (migration path if affirmed).\n190\t- Without RHAISTRAT-7164, user-facing value still ships via BRANCH-B-E006 (BFF-managed,\n191\t  preserves the out-of-the-box experience) or, as the degraded fallback, BRANCH-C-E006\n192\t  (customer-provided PostgreSQL). The operator-based approach is Tier 2, not a linear\n193\t  blocker of all downstream work.\n194\t\n195\t## Component Mapping Notes\n196\t\n197\t- Every epic uses the canonical component **Gen AI Studio** from\n198\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt`. BRANCH-A-E006 spans the dashboard operator layer\n199\t  (an AI Core Dashboard surface delivered by RHAISTRAT-7164); the single most-relevant\n200\t  canonical name for the delivered capability is Gen AI Studio, so the span is noted\n201\t  here rather than splitting the epic across components.\n202\t- **OGX distribution is passive** (no code change): the remote PGVector vector I/O\n203\t  backend already exists — corroborated by\n204\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md`\n205\t  (vector I/O through Milvus, PGVector, or Qdrant backends; inline providers stripped\n206\t  from the distribution build). Provider behavior is validated as acceptance criteria on\n207\t  the delivery branches.\n208\t- **Team assignments** (Gen AI Studio for all epics) are inferred from the Staff Engineer\n209\t  Input because the Impacted Teams table is unavailable (Health Warning #1).\n210\t\n211\t## Conditional Branches\n212\t\n213\t- **E001 (go/no-go) outcomes:** A — RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time → operator-based\n214\t  self-provisioning (BRANCH-A-E006); B — operator layer slips → BFF-managed provisioning\n215\t  (BRANCH-B-E006, the preferred alternative per SE input); C — operator layer slips and\n216\t  BFF-managed is ruled out → customer-provided PostgreSQL (BRANCH-C-E006). Exactly one\n217\t  branch is implemented.\n218\t- **E003 (migration path) outcomes:** A — migration path affirmed → BRANCH-A-E007;\n219\t  not affirmed → no epic (positioning guidance only, folded into the selected delivery\n220\t  branch's documentation scope).\n221\t- Branch epics are not standalone Jira issues; each branch's epics attach to the gating\n222\t  Investigation as a plan document. Branch epics are excluded from the main-plan\n223\t  critical path. `epic_count` counts all epics including branches (9); main-plan epics:\n224\t  5; main-plan critical path length: 2.\n225\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-99a250d43d96258d","agent_id":"a46add88d99ec933a","session_id":"172d32aa-435a-4a07-8b59-185510da7cc9","uuid":"04be3b2d-1407-49e4-8ee0-4b592eb83ca7","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:38:50.871Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bf803a33547e0a10","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","rea