issing\n100\t  dependency edge discoverable from epic content, and it is internally inconsistent with\n101\t  the decomposition's own treatment of E005. Correction: add `RHAISTRAT-8004-E001` to\n102\t  BRANCH-A-E007's `dependencies` (retaining the delivery coupling as an AC), mirroring\n103\t  E005.\n104\t\n105\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n106\t\n107\tNon-issues first: BRANCH-A-E006 and BRANCH-B-E006 exceed the ~2-week guideline but are\n108\tshape-(b) compliant — Health Warning #5 records the estimate, the why-not-split\n109\trationale, and a concrete conditional re-split trigger, so no split is demanded. The\n110\tthree E006 branches are mutually exclusive conditional alternatives, not scope\n111\tduplication. BRANCH-A-E006's span across the operator layer and BFF wiring is one\n112\tlogical change, documented in Component Mapping Notes.\n113\t\n114\tOne major issue:\n115\t\n116\t- **Work-product type mixing.** BRANCH-C-E006 bundles code implementation\n117\t  (user-supplied connection support, playground validation feedback) with substantive\n118\t  user-documentation authoring — a new end-to-end PostgreSQL setup guide carrying its\n119\t  own acceptance criterion (\"Documentation covers the setup end to end with no\n120\t  undocumented manual steps\"). BRANCH-A-E007 does the same (migration implementation plus\n121\t  user documentation for the migration path, with two documentation-specific ACs). Code\n122\t  implementation and content/documentation authoring are different kinds of work with\n123\t  different review criteria; they belong in separate epics even within the same branch\n124\t  and team. Correction: split the documentation work out of both epics (e.g., a\n125\t  docs-authoring epic inside each affected branch, or per Rule 11), leaving only\n126\t  incidental doc touch-ups in the code epics.\n127\t\n128\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n129\t\n130\tAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's outcome selects\n131\tone of three implementation branches (existence), E002's outcome rewrites what the\n132\tbranches and validation build (scope), E003's outcome determines whether BRANCH-A-E007\n133\texists. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (decision records, benchmark\n134\tharness/report, provisioned capability, validation report). Every epic with `gated_by`\n135\thas a populated `gate_failure_impact` (action + fallback_approach), and every `gated_by`\n136\ttarget appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`.\n137\t\n138\tOne minor issue:\n139\t\n140\t- E002 is a second true gate (rewrite semantics — \"the scoping model rewrites what each\n141\t  provisioning branch implements\") for E005 and all three branch E006 epics, but\n142\t  `gated_by` (single-valued) points only at E001; the E002-outcome coupling is recorded\n143\t  only inside `gate_failure_impact` prose and is therefore not machine-readable as a\n144\t  gate. Consider recording the E002 gate relationship explicitly (e.g., in the\n145\t  decomposition summary's DAG justification as a named gate, or restructuring so the\n146\t  primary gate covers it) so automated consumers can detect it.\n147\t\n148\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (0/2)\n149\t\n150\tType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout (Investigation epics carry\n151\t`investigation_signals`, Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`); all nine ai-signals\n152\tfiles exist with detailed rationales; `architecture_claims: +1` values cite real,\n153\tverifiable context files (ogx-distribution.md documents vector I/O through\n154\tMilvus/PGVector/Qdrant; model-registry-operator.md documents the per-instance\n155\t`{registry-name}-postgres` provisioning pattern; odh-dashboard.md documents the gen-ai\n156\tBFF). E001's signals are well calibrated (notably `source_accessibility: 0` because the\n157\tdetermining fact — RHAISTRAT-7164 timing — does not exist in any readable artifact).\n158\t\n159\tOne major issue:\n160\t\n161\t- **E003 scores `source_accessibility: +1`, contradicting the rubric.** The +1 condition\n162\t  requires a readable artifact that contains or determines the answer. E003's own\n163\t  `human_judgment_required: -2` rationale states the gating question — whether users\n164\t  need a migration path — \"is a product decision... the AI cannot make the call.\" A\n165\t  pending human/product decision is not determined by any readable artifact; the\n166\t  ai-signals file's rationale (\"what a migration would involve is determinable from\n167\t  public repos and docs\") describes the technical sub-facts, not the answer. E001 scored\n168\t  0 for exactly this shape (\"repo/code facts about the fallbacks are readable, but they\n169\t  do not determine the answer\"). The miscalibration is material: the correct sum is -1\n170\t  (routing Low, assign to a person) instead of the recorded 0 (Medium, hybrid).\n171\t  Correction: re-score `source_accessibility` to 0 and rewrite the rationale to name\n172\t  what is and is not readable.\n173\t\n174\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n175\t\n176\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs\n177\tare present where applicable: the delivery branches carry escape-hatch/rollback ACs\n178\t(\"the playground can be pointed at an externally provisioned vector database\") for the\n179\tcapability replacement; BRANCH-C-E006 is itself the external-database path so no\n180\trollback AC applies; no konflux chain exists (Correction 2), and there are no\n181\tdocs-authoring epics (see Criterion 3 for where the doc work went). E005's first AC\n182\t(\"validated against an instance delivered by the selected provisioning approach\") is\n183\tthe endorsed branch-convention coupling AC on the epic's own deliverable, not a misfiled\n184\texternal precondition. Schedule/availability risks (RHAISTRAT-7164 timing, baseline\n185\tcapture before RHAISTRAT-7474) are correctly filed in Risks/Notes and Health Warnings\n186\trather than as ACs. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n187\t\n188\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n189\t\n190\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: every Acceptance Criterion and Success Criterion\n191\tmaps into the requirement inventory and epic set, with REQ-3 struck under a logged Staff\n192\tEngineer override. Strategy-level context is carried forward rather than silently\n193\tdropped: the RHAISTRAT-7164 timing risk (E001 + Health Warning #2), the baseline-capture\n194\ttiming risk (E004 Risks/Notes + Health Warning #3), the uncorroborated image-content\n195\tclaims (Health Warning #6 with a verification instruction), and both stakeholders'\n196\tpositions (per-namespace preference → E002; migration-provider alignment → E003).\n197\tConditional branches cover all bounded outcomes for both gates (E001: A/B/C; E003:\n198\taffirmed → BRANCH-A-E007, not affirmed → documentation-only, folded into the selected\n199\tdelivery). Cross-epic consistency holds: the branch set collectively covers all\n200\tprovisioning outcomes including the degraded one, and BRANCH-C-E006's deliberate partial\n201\tcoverage of the out-of-the-box requirement is explicit rather than silent. Caveat on\n202\trecord: the Prerequisites & Process Gates section is in the missing attachment, so the\n203\tDocumentation Support / Rule 11 docs-authoring requirement cannot be verified from the\n204\tavailable inputs — this rides on Health Warning #1 and should be re-checked when the\n205\tattachment is available.\n206\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6e723cc7b26cd0d","agent_id":"aa8f64a754387b1a3","session_id":"172d32aa-435a-4a07-8b59-185510da7cc9","uuid":"4fa22eec-0ecf-4f34-98a1-66f3a37ff12d","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:46:06.215Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a38673885f4570dd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
