rategy's scope, the per-instance scoping\n177\t   option and an Auto Rag validation epic need re-evaluation (see E002).\n178\t5. **Epic size above the ~2-week guideline (managed).** BRANCH-A-E006 and\n179\t   BRANCH-B-E006 are each estimated at ~1.5-2 sprints (the strategy's own effort\n180\t   arithmetic is in the missing attachment, so these are qualitative). Kept whole\n181\t   because each is one logical change — provision and wire the default store for one\n182\t   approach — sharing the same code paths, CRD/BFF surfaces, and review cycle; splitting\n183\t   provisioning from provider wiring would build the same surface twice and add an\n184\t   artificial serialization edge. Re-split trigger: if the lifecycle-design work\n185\t   (cleanup on deletion, pod-restart handling, credential rotation) exceeds its initial\n186\t   design estimate by more than ~50%, split lifecycle hardening into a follow-up epic\n187\t   inside the same branch.\n188\t6. **Architecture-context gap on the image claim.** The SE's claims that\n189\t   `rhel9/postgresql-16` ships pgvector and that `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` is\n190\t   already in the rhods-operator CSV are not corroborated by the available\n191\t   architecture-context files (the model-registry-operator context does corroborate\n192\t   operator-pattern PostgreSQL auto-provisioning with per-instance postgres deployments).\n193\t   No epic depends on the image-build claim — no image-build work exists in this\n194\t   decomposition — but verify the image contents during branch implementation.\n195\t\n196\t## Tiered Delivery\n197\t\n198\tThe external dependency with uncertain timing (RHAISTRAT-7164, another in-progress\n199\tstrategy) was evaluated for tiered delivery per Step 5.5. Partial value IS deliverable\n200\twithout it:\n201\t\n202\t- **Tier 1 (independent, starts immediately):** E001, E002, E003 (decisions) and E004\n203\t  (risk-reduction benchmark — captures the inline baseline before inline-provider remova