{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8cf65acf831a4864","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E006\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed provisioning of the default pgvector vector store\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: remove\n16\t  fallback_approach: 'If the go/no-go decision selects the operator-based approach\n17\t    or customer-provided PostgreSQL, this epic is not implemented; only the external-database\n18\t    escape-hatch support from the selected branch may be retained. The scoping-model\n19\t    investigation is a second true gate with rewrite semantics for this epic: if it\n20\t    lands on a different model, the provisioning topology (instance placement, isolation,\n21\t    lifecycle ownership) is rewritten to match.'\n22\tai_signals:\n23\t  change_specificity: 1\n24\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n25\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n26\t  existing_foundation: 1\n27\t  open_questions: -1\n28\t  external_dependency: 1\n29\t  human_process_gates: 0\n30\t  repo_access: 1\n31\t  architecture_claims: 1\n32\timplementation_type: null\n33\tinvestigation_signals: null\n34\tai_implementability: High\n35\tai_implementability_score: 5\n36\tjira_key: null\n37\t---\n38\t# BFF-Managed Provisioning of the Default pgvector Vector Store\n39\t\n40\t## Description\n41\t\n42\tFallback delivery of the default vector store when the RHOAI-controlled operator layer is not available in time for RHOAI 3.5: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly — creating the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured, if one does not already exist in the namespace — similar to how the BFF currently provisions guardrails infrastructure. The BFF also configures playground OGXServer instances to use the remote pgvector vector I/O provider backed by that instance, preserving the out-of-the-box experience without the operator layer.\n43\t\n44\t## Scope\n45\t\n46\t- BFF-managed provisioning of PostgreSQL using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image that ships pgvector (no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding): create the Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured and no instance exists in the namespace.\n47\t- Explicit lifecycle design and implementation — the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, and credential rotation need explicit design attention.\n48\t- Portability by design: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning; the connection-resolution path is abstracted accordingly.\n49\t- Implement the scoping model selected by the scoping investigation (per-namespace isolation preferred).\n50\t- Resource requests/limits start from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing and are adjusted per the benchmark findings.\n51\t- OGXServer wiring: playground OGXServer instances use the remote pgvector vector I/O provider backed by the provisioned instance; OGX application storage remains on its existing SQLite default (independent configurations — no consolidation).\n52\t- Reuse the existing OGX remote vector store implementation; no OGX distribution code change expected.\n53\t- Document the default store's positioning: development-experimentation grade, with production-scale workloads directed to an external vector database.\n54\t\n55\t## Acceptance Criteria\n56\t\n57\t- Configuring a playground with RAG provisions a PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret) in the namespace when none exists, and the OGX vector I/O provider is configured to use it.\n58\t- Lifecycle behaviors — cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart handling, credential rotation — are designed and implemented per the lifecycle design.\n59\t- Portability: the connection-resolution path is abstracted so a platform-provided shared connection string can be consumed in RHOAI 3.6 without rework.\n60\t- Scoping follows the selected scoping model; resource requests/limits follow the model-registry-operator reference, adjusted per the benchmark findings.\n61\t- Escape hatch / rollback path: the playground can be pointed at an externally provisioned vector database.\n62\t- OGX application storage remains SQLite; no vector/application storage consolidation is introduced.\n63\t\n64\t## HLR Traceability\n65\t\n66\t- REQ-1: default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning or configuration.\n67\t- REQ-2: playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store.\n68\t- REQ-4: default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance.\n69\t- REQ-5: multi-tenant scoping per the selected model.\n70\t- REQ-6: lightweight resource footprint.\n71\t- REQ-10: portability toward the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided shared PostgreSQL (explicit design-for-portability requirement).\n72\t\n73\t## Risks / Notes\n74\t\n75\t- This branch exists only if the go/no-go decision selects the BFF-managed fallback (operator layer not ready in time).\n76\t- Epic size: ~1.5-2 sprints (qualitative — the strategy's effort arithmetic is unavailable); kept whole because BFF provisioning and provider wiring form a single logical change over the same BFF code paths. Re-split trigger: the lifecycle-design work (cleanup, pod-restart handling, credential rotation) exceeds its design estimate by more than ~50%, at which point lifecycle hardening splits into a follow-up epic within this branch.\n77\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-86f5e150208d747e","agent_id":"a5236c8cb9613bd11","session_id":"172d32aa-435a-4a07-8b59-185510da7cc9"